{"step_index":3314,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T17:11:56Z","thinking":"Successfully processed two JSON files, confirming the complete extraction and saving of their respective data. Now, the focus shifts to understanding the file reading process within the `file_handler.py` module to analyze its behavior.\n\n\nThe current implementation processes files sequentially, leading to significant delays and vulnerability to failures. Each file must complete before the next begins. Should a file fail or timeout, the entire process halts, discarding partial results.\n\nConsidering an alternative, parallel execution with a thread pool executor would allow concurrent file processing. This would significantly reduce overall processing time and improve resilience against individual file failures.\n\n\nParallelizing the file processing using a thread pool executor will allow for concurrent handling of different file types. This approach anticipates significant time savings, as the total processing duration will be determined by the longest-running individual file, rather than the cumulative time for all files.\n\nFurthermore, this concurrent design incorporates fault tolerance; if a single file processing thread encounters an error, such as a timeout, it will fail gracefully. The unaffected files will still be processed and the pipeline will continue using the successfully processed data.\n\n\nThe initial timeout observed with the PDF, which has 22 pages, required further investigation to understand why the Unstructured process failed. Direct text extraction using a different method was considered. Previous attempts to extract text from the PDF directly with a library revealed reversed Arabic text glyphs, indicating a potential issue specific to legacy pharmacy ERP software's output format. This may explain why OCR was required, and the timeout. The timeout setting for the Unstructured process will be examined.\n\n\n","tool_ca